1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,280
A woman sits at a desk with a
sheet of paper rolled into a heavy

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:04,350
black machine.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,710
She presses a key, a
metal arm swings up,

4
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:09,610
and a letter lands on the page.

5
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:12,270
At the end of the line,
a small bell rings.

6
00:00:12,894 --> 00:00:15,324
It sounds like an
ordinary office noise.

7
00:00:15,674 --> 00:00:18,834
But the first typewriter that
sold in any number looked more

8
00:00:18,874 --> 00:00:22,464
like a sewing machine, and it
could only print capital letters.

9
00:00:23,301 --> 00:00:25,171
This is the story of the typewriter,

10
00:00:25,341 --> 00:00:29,261
and how a page-numbering idea
from a Milwaukee workshop ended

11
00:00:29,301 --> 00:00:31,251
up on desks all over the world.

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,210
Before the typewriter,
every business letter,

13
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:43,010
ledger and contract
was written by hand.

14
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:46,580
If you wanted a clean copy, a
clerk wrote it out again with a

15
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:47,050
pen.

16
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:49,240
Handwriting could be beautiful,

17
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,760
but it was slow, and it was only
as readable as the person who

18
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:53,450
wrote it.

19
00:00:53,822 --> 00:00:55,352
In eighteen sixty-six,

20
00:00:55,722 --> 00:00:59,212
in a converted mill that served
as a machine shop in Milwaukee,

21
00:00:59,602 --> 00:01:03,322
Wisconsin, an inventor named
Christopher Sholes and his friend

22
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:06,902
Carlos Glidden were working on
a device that numbered the pages

23
00:01:06,942 --> 00:01:07,772
of books.

24
00:01:08,462 --> 00:01:12,122
Sholes and Soule had already received
a patent for a page-numbering

25
00:01:12,162 --> 00:01:14,632
device that same year, in November.

26
00:01:14,822 --> 00:01:17,692
The story goes that they then
asked a bigger question.

27
00:01:18,142 --> 00:01:20,132
Why not a machine that prints words?

28
00:01:20,602 --> 00:01:22,512
Sholes had asked a third man,

29
00:01:22,602 --> 00:01:24,792
Samuel Soule, to join the work,

30
00:01:24,882 --> 00:01:27,682
and the three of them kept tinkering
until the numbering machine

31
00:01:27,722 --> 00:01:28,812
could print letters.

32
00:01:29,618 --> 00:01:31,878
On the twenty-third of
June, eighteen sixty-eight,

33
00:01:31,918 --> 00:01:35,318
the United States granted patent
number seventy-nine thousand

34
00:01:35,358 --> 00:01:38,578
two hundred sixty-five,
for a type-writing machine,

35
00:01:38,618 --> 00:01:41,128
to Sholes, Glidden and Soule.

36
00:01:41,598 --> 00:01:44,138
One source says it was
filed earlier that month,

37
00:01:44,178 --> 00:01:45,628
on the third of June.

38
00:01:46,153 --> 00:01:49,883
We found no film of Sholes's
workshop or of his machine.

39
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:52,923
The pictures in this
chapter are stand-ins:

40
00:01:53,193 --> 00:01:56,503
typing scenes from films made
about eighty years later,

41
00:01:56,573 --> 00:01:59,303
so every machine you
see here is a later one.

42
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:07,723
A patent is not a product.

43
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:11,203
For several years the machine
stayed a workshop curiosity,

44
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:13,303
and it needed money and a factory.

45
00:02:14,013 --> 00:02:16,980
In eighteen seventy-two, the patent
was sold for twelve thousand

46
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:20,463
dollars to two businessmen,
Densmore and Yost.

47
00:02:20,693 --> 00:02:22,183
They took the machine to E.

48
00:02:22,813 --> 00:02:24,713
Remington and Sons, in Ilion,

49
00:02:24,753 --> 00:02:28,623
New York, a company better known
for rifles and sewing machines.

50
00:02:29,064 --> 00:02:32,634
Remington's engineers had a sewing
machine business to draw on,

51
00:02:32,724 --> 00:02:36,334
and their answer was to dress the
typewriter like a sewing machine.

52
00:02:36,804 --> 00:02:39,844
Early models stood on a stand
with a foot treadle to return the

53
00:02:39,884 --> 00:02:43,674
carriage, and the black case was
decorated with painted flowers.

54
00:02:44,124 --> 00:02:46,054
It was meant to look
at home in an office,

55
00:02:46,224 --> 00:02:47,434
and in a parlor.

56
00:02:48,064 --> 00:02:51,144
One source says the treadle was
replaced by a hand lever around

57
00:02:51,184 --> 00:02:53,574
eighteen seventy-six or seventy-seven.

58
00:02:54,294 --> 00:02:56,144
The dates differ between sources.

59
00:02:56,714 --> 00:03:00,004
One says Remington began
production on the first of March,

60
00:03:00,254 --> 00:03:01,524
eighteen seventy-three.

61
00:03:02,094 --> 00:03:04,254
Another treats that as
the date of a contract,

62
00:03:04,294 --> 00:03:08,664
with the machines reaching shops in
the summer of eighteen seventy-four.

63
00:03:09,054 --> 00:03:11,904
So we say eighteen
seventy-three to seventy-four.

64
00:03:12,634 --> 00:03:15,764
We could not find film of these
first Remington machines either,

65
00:03:15,914 --> 00:03:18,024
so the pictures here are, again,

66
00:03:18,174 --> 00:03:19,084
stand-ins.

67
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:26,963
Then there was the keyboard.

68
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:29,943
Every typewriter works
the same basic way.

69
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:33,773
Press a key, and a metal bar
carrying one letter swings up and

70
00:03:33,813 --> 00:03:36,043
strikes an inked ribbon
against the paper.

71
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:39,183
The carriage then moves
along by one space,

72
00:03:39,393 --> 00:03:40,663
ready for the next letter.

73
00:03:40,993 --> 00:03:44,313
The first machines had metal
bars that swung up to strike the

74
00:03:44,353 --> 00:03:48,143
page, and when two neighboring bars
were pressed in quick succession,

75
00:03:48,413 --> 00:03:49,203
they could jam.

76
00:03:49,917 --> 00:03:52,657
The usual story says the letters
were scattered so that common

77
00:03:52,697 --> 00:03:55,647
pairs sat far apart,
to reduce jamming.

78
00:03:55,897 --> 00:03:58,347
That gave us the top
row that begins Q,

79
00:03:58,657 --> 00:04:01,327
W, E, R, T, Y.

80
00:04:01,777 --> 00:04:04,537
One account adds that Remington
later moved a single key,

81
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:08,117
so that a salesman could spell
the word typewriter using only

82
00:04:08,157 --> 00:04:09,087
the top row.

83
00:04:09,697 --> 00:04:12,657
Historians still argue about
how much of the story is true,

84
00:04:12,697 --> 00:04:15,047
and we will not pretend to settle it.

85
00:04:21,437 --> 00:04:24,047
What we do know is that
sales started slowly.

86
00:04:24,217 --> 00:04:27,917
By one count, about five thousand
of these Sholes and Glidden

87
00:04:27,957 --> 00:04:31,777
machines were sold between
eighteen seventy-four and eighteen

88
00:04:31,817 --> 00:04:32,587
seventy-eight.

89
00:04:33,077 --> 00:04:36,927
Another account says about four
thousand by eighteen seventy-seven.

90
00:04:37,658 --> 00:04:39,768
The price was about one
hundred twenty-five dollars,

91
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:44,108
which one account says was
roughly a year's average income.

92
00:04:44,278 --> 00:04:46,948
And the machine printed
only capital letters.

93
00:04:47,358 --> 00:04:50,538
By one account, the typist could
not even see the line being

94
00:04:50,578 --> 00:04:54,208
typed, because the bars struck
the underside of the paper.

95
00:04:54,652 --> 00:04:56,712
That made it good for
headings and short notes,

96
00:04:56,752 --> 00:04:59,462
and awkward for almost
everything else.

97
00:04:59,832 --> 00:05:03,292
By one account, Remington cut
the price to eighty dollars in

98
00:05:03,332 --> 00:05:06,122
eighteen eighty-one, as
competitors arrived.

99
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,840
In eighteen seventy-eight,
Remington brought out the Remington

100
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:16,790
Number Two,

101
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:20,230
and it fixed the capital letter
problem with one new key.

102
00:05:20,762 --> 00:05:22,252
This was the shift key.

103
00:05:22,602 --> 00:05:25,892
Hold it down, and each bar
printed a second character.

104
00:05:26,362 --> 00:05:29,752
The same keys now gave both
capital and small letters,

105
00:05:29,962 --> 00:05:33,232
and the machine needed far fewer
keys than it would have otherwise.

106
00:05:37,759 --> 00:05:40,389
Some sources place
the shift key earlier,

107
00:05:40,579 --> 00:05:42,149
around eighteen seventy-four.

108
00:05:42,639 --> 00:05:44,709
We went with eighteen seventy-eight,

109
00:05:44,939 --> 00:05:47,839
the year given for the Number
Two by the sources we could check

110
00:05:47,879 --> 00:05:48,689
twice.

111
00:05:49,221 --> 00:05:51,191
Typing took on its familiar rhythm,

112
00:05:51,421 --> 00:05:54,571
and the instructional films
we use show that rhythm well,

113
00:05:54,701 --> 00:05:58,211
with hands moving over the keys
and the carriage sliding along.

114
00:05:58,661 --> 00:06:01,861
A small bell rang a few characters
before the right edge of the

115
00:06:01,901 --> 00:06:02,571
page.

116
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:04,571
The typist finished the word,

117
00:06:04,781 --> 00:06:06,391
pushed the carriage return lever,

118
00:06:06,601 --> 00:06:07,971
and started the next line.

119
00:06:08,444 --> 00:06:11,774
The bell you hear in this
film is a synthesized tone,

120
00:06:11,984 --> 00:06:15,014
because we could not find a
recording we were allowed to use.

121
00:06:15,444 --> 00:06:19,294
The key sounds are real recordings
of a typewriter in use,

122
00:06:19,604 --> 00:06:22,744
and the film clips are from
instructional films of the nineteen

123
00:06:22,784 --> 00:06:25,534
forties, which show later
machines than these.

124
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:36,310
By the end of the century, the
typewriter was no longer a curiosity.

125
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:40,580
By nineteen hundred, as many as
one hundred thousand typewriters

126
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:42,330
were sold in a single year.

127
00:06:42,745 --> 00:06:44,715
Compare that with the first machines.

128
00:06:45,125 --> 00:06:47,975
About five thousand over
four or five years,

129
00:06:48,105 --> 00:06:50,535
then about one hundred
thousand in one year.

130
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:52,895
The two numbers are
counted differently,

131
00:06:53,085 --> 00:06:54,555
but the direction is clear.

132
00:06:55,013 --> 00:06:57,783
The machine also changed
who worked in offices.

133
00:06:58,193 --> 00:07:01,583
Remington styled its machines
like sewing machines,

134
00:07:01,773 --> 00:07:04,403
and offices began to
hire women to run them.

135
00:07:04,813 --> 00:07:07,263
One account says that
in eighteen seventy,

136
00:07:07,553 --> 00:07:10,713
women made up about two and a
half percent of American clerical

137
00:07:10,753 --> 00:07:13,023
workers, and that by nineteen thirty,

138
00:07:13,313 --> 00:07:14,763
they made up more than half.

139
00:07:15,013 --> 00:07:17,903
Other details from that
account are small but telling.

140
00:07:18,453 --> 00:07:22,413
A big credit firm began hiring
women into its offices in eighteen

141
00:07:22,453 --> 00:07:25,043
seventy-five, and in
eighteen eighty-one,

142
00:07:25,353 --> 00:07:28,773
a women's association in New
York opened a typewriting course

143
00:07:28,813 --> 00:07:32,043
for eight young women, which
some preachers denounced.

144
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:36,043
An economic historian quoted there
calls the typewriter crucial,

145
00:07:36,313 --> 00:07:37,683
but not the only key.

146
00:07:38,182 --> 00:07:40,892
For the person at the desk,
the appeal was simple.

147
00:07:41,302 --> 00:07:43,412
A typed page was easy to read,

148
00:07:43,522 --> 00:07:46,352
and an operator could
write faster than by hand.

149
00:07:46,762 --> 00:07:50,062
One writer in eighteen eighty-eight
said typing paid as well

150
00:07:50,102 --> 00:07:53,662
as teaching, which was then the
main respectable profession open

151
00:07:53,702 --> 00:07:57,202
to women, and he guessed that a
typewriter could save forty minutes

152
00:07:57,242 --> 00:07:58,792
in every hour of writing.

153
00:07:59,222 --> 00:08:02,032
That was one man's
estimate, not a measurement.

154
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:06,380
A Milwaukee workshop set out to
number pages and ended up with

155
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:07,070
a keyboard.

156
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:11,040
A company that made rifles and
sewing machines turned it into

157
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:14,790
a product, a shift key made
it a real writing machine,

158
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,940
and by nineteen hundred it was
selling by the hundred thousand

159
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:18,590
a year.

160
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:22,110
Along the way, it helped open
office work to many more people,

161
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:25,570
and made the written page quicker
to produce and easier to read.

162
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:29,630
And today, the letters on your
phone still begin with Q,

163
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:35,900
W, E, R, T, Y, a small reminder
of a machine built to keep its

164
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:37,530
metal bars from tangling.

165
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:39,330
Ordinary objects.

166
00:08:39,540 --> 00:08:40,870
Extraordinary stories.
